{
"duration": 661.2,
"lines": [
{
"i": 0,
"p": 0,
"text": "Right now, there's a number on your phone that decides what you can eat this week.",
"start": 0.437,
"end": 4.875
},
{
"i": 1,
"p": 0,
"text": "Your bank balance.",
"start": 6.152,
"end": 7.218
},
{
"i": 2,
"p": 0,
"text": "You check it.",
"start": 8.427,
"end": 9.245
},
{
"i": 3,
"p": 0,
"text": "You worry about it.",
"start": 10.444,
"end": 11.514
},
{
"i": 4,
"p": 0,
"text": "You work forty hours a week to make it go up.",
"start": 12.749,
"end": 15.259
},
{
"i": 5,
"p": 1,
"text": "But for almost all of human history, that number didn't exist.",
"start": 16.997,
"end": 20.936
},
{
"i": 6,
"p": 1,
"text": "No coins.",
"start": 22.204,
"end": 23.078
},
{
"i": 7,
"p": 1,
"text": "No banks.",
"start": 24.276,
"end": 25.094
},
{
"i": 8,
"p": 1,
"text": "No bills.",
"start": 26.31,
"end": 27.127
},
{
"i": 9,
"p": 1,
"text": "For hundreds of thousands of years, humans lived with no money at all.",
"start": 28.351,
"end": 32.583
},
{
"i": 10,
"p": 2,
"text": "So how did they get anything?",
"start": 34.361,
"end": 35.818
},
{
"i": 11,
"p": 3,
"text": "You probably learned the answer in school.",
"start": 37.607,
"end": 39.629
},
{
"i": 12,
"p": 3,
"text": "They bartered.",
"start": 40.949,
"end": 41.767
},
{
"i": 13,
"p": 3,
"text": "I'll give you three fish for your spear.",
"start": 42.981,
"end": 44.875
},
{
"i": 14,
"p": 3,
"text": "Then barter got annoying, so someone invented money.",
"start": 46.193,
"end": 49.465
},
{
"i": 15,
"p": 4,
"text": "It's a great story.",
"start": 51.308,
"end": 52.399
},
{
"i": 16,
"p": 4,
"text": "There's just one problem.",
"start": 53.654,
"end": 55.041
},
{
"i": 17,
"p": 4,
"text": "It almost certainly never happened.",
"start": 56.343,
"end": 58.224
},
{
"i": 18,
"p": 5,
"text": "Chapter one.",
"start": 59.947,
"end": 60.826
},
{
"i": 19,
"p": 5,
"text": "The barter myth.",
"start": 62.037,
"end": 63.018
},
{
"i": 20,
"p": 6,
"text": "Here's a test.",
"start": 64.678,
"end": 65.594
},
{
"i": 21,
"p": 6,
"text": "Two hunters.",
"start": 66.83,
"end": 67.711
},
{
"i": 22,
"p": 6,
"text": "One has meat.",
"start": 68.944,
"end": 69.891
},
{
"i": 23,
"p": 6,
"text": "The other has a brand new spear.",
"start": 71.115,
"end": 72.864
},
{
"i": 24,
"p": 6,
"text": "The first one wants the spear.",
"start": 74.214,
"end": 75.869
},
{
"i": 25,
"p": 6,
"text": "But the second one isn't hungry.",
"start": 77.184,
"end": 78.972
},
{
"i": 26,
"p": 6,
"text": "He wants a warm fur.",
"start": 80.347,
"end": 81.631
},
{
"i": 27,
"p": 6,
"text": "So... what happens?",
"start": 82.93,
"end": 85.488
},
{
"i": 28,
"p": 7,
"text": "In the barter story, nothing.",
"start": 87.175,
"end": 88.655
},
{
"i": 29,
"p": 7,
"text": "No deal.",
"start": 89.993,
"end": 90.698
},
{
"i": 30,
"p": 7,
"text": "Everyone goes home angry.",
"start": 91.989,
"end": 93.504
},
{
"i": 31,
"p": 7,
"text": "Economists even have a name for this problem: the double coincidence of wants.",
"start": 94.897,
"end": 99.859
},
{
"i": 32,
"p": 8,
"text": "The story comes from Adam Smith, in seventeen seventy-six.",
"start": 101.553,
"end": 105.5
},
{
"i": 33,
"p": 8,
"text": "A baker has bread.",
"start": 106.758,
"end": 107.921
},
{
"i": 34,
"p": 8,
"text": "A butcher has meat.",
"start": 109.215,
"end": 110.363
},
{
"i": 35,
"p": 8,
"text": "But the butcher already has bread.",
"start": 111.638,
"end": 113.321
},
{
"i": 36,
"p": 8,
"text": "So no exchange can be made.",
"start": 114.624,
"end": 116.328
},
{
"i": 37,
"p": 9,
"text": "Anthropologists went looking for these barter economies.",
"start": 118.099,
"end": 121.308
},
{
"i": 38,
"p": 9,
"text": "All over the world.",
"start": 122.602,
"end": 123.694
},
{
"i": 39,
"p": 9,
"text": "For more than a century.",
"start": 124.931,
"end": 126.218
},
{
"i": 40,
"p": 10,
"text": "In nineteen eighty-five, Cambridge anthropologist Caroline Humphrey summed up what they found.",
"start": 127.997,
"end": 133.923
},
{
"i": 41,
"p": 10,
"text": "No example of a barter economy, pure and simple, has ever been described.",
"start": 135.274,
"end": 140.448
},
{
"i": 42,
"p": 11,
"text": "Not one.",
"start": 142.181,
"end": 142.902
},
{
"i": 43,
"p": 11,
"text": "So if they didn't barter... what did they actually do?",
"start": 144.097,
"end": 148.225
},
{
"i": 44,
"p": 12,
"text": "Chapter two.",
"start": 149.993,
"end": 150.894
},
{
"i": 45,
"p": 12,
"text": "The gift.",
"start": 152.057,
"end": 152.723
},
{
"i": 46,
"p": 13,
"text": "Picture a camp in the Kalahari, thousands of years ago.",
"start": 154.324,
"end": 157.741
},
{
"i": 47,
"p": 13,
"text": "A hunter comes home with a big antelope.",
"start": 159.138,
"end": 161.454
},
{
"i": 48,
"p": 14,
"text": "He doesn't sell it.",
"start": 163.183,
"end": 164.113
},
{
"i": 49,
"p": 14,
"text": "He doesn't trade it.",
"start": 165.421,
"end": 166.373
},
{
"i": 50,
"p": 14,
"text": "He shares it.",
"start": 167.66,
"end": 168.403
},
{
"i": 51,
"p": 14,
"text": "With everyone.",
"start": 169.681,
"end": 170.576
},
{
"i": 52,
"p": 15,
"text": "Why would anyone give away the most valuable thing they own?",
"start": 172.28,
"end": 175.54
},
{
"i": 53,
"p": 15,
"text": "Because next week, he might come home with nothing.",
"start": 177.009,
"end": 179.611
},
{
"i": 54,
"p": 15,
"text": "And then someone else shares with him.",
"start": 180.973,
"end": 182.731
},
{
"i": 55,
"p": 16,
"text": "Among the Zhu-twa-see people of southern Africa, anthropologist Polly Wiessner studied a gift system called kharo.",
"start": 184.562,
"end": 192.012
},
{
"i": 56,
"p": 16,
"text": "People gave beads, arrows and tools to partners who lived far away.",
"start": 193.422,
"end": 197.701
},
{
"i": 57,
"p": 16,
"text": "Sometimes they waited months, or years, for a gift in return.",
"start": 199.065,
"end": 202.893
},
{
"i": 58,
"p": 17,
"text": "That wasn't generosity.",
"start": 204.766,
"end": 206.299
},
{
"i": 59,
"p": 17,
"text": "It was insurance.",
"start": 207.648,
"end": 208.748
},
{
"i": 60,
"p": 17,
"text": "If drought hit your land, you could walk to your kharo partner's camp... and they had to take you in.",
"start": 210.014,
"end": 216.683
},
{
"i": 61,
"p": 18,
"text": "So the first \"money\" wasn't a thing.",
"start": 218.48,
"end": 220.383
},
{
"i": 62,
"p": 18,
"text": "It was a relationship.",
"start": 221.781,
"end": 223.114
},
{
"i": 63,
"p": 18,
"text": "A promise.",
"start": 224.36,
"end": 225.148
},
{
"i": 64,
"p": 18,
"text": "I'll remember what you did for me.",
"start": 226.397,
"end": 228.205
},
{
"i": 65,
"p": 19,
"text": "Chapter three.",
"start": 229.986,
"end": 230.91
},
{
"i": 66,
"p": 19,
"text": "The beads.",
"start": 232.105,
"end": 232.881
},
{
"i": 67,
"p": 20,
"text": "In a cave on the coast of South Africa, called Blombos Cave, archaeologists found something strange.",
"start": 234.534,
"end": 240.929
},
{
"i": 68,
"p": 20,
"text": "Tiny sea shells, each with a hole in it.",
"start": 242.214,
"end": 244.452
},
{
"i": 69,
"p": 20,
"text": "Strung together.",
"start": 245.781,
"end": 246.703
},
{
"i": 70,
"p": 20,
"text": "Seventy-five thousand years old.",
"start": 247.942,
"end": 249.96
},
{
"i": 71,
"p": 21,
"text": "They're some of the oldest jewelry ever found.",
"start": 251.694,
"end": 254.139
},
{
"i": 72,
"p": 21,
"text": "And jewelry is interesting.",
"start": 255.441,
"end": 256.979
},
{
"i": 73,
"p": 21,
"text": "You can't eat it.",
"start": 258.33,
"end": 259.273
},
{
"i": 74,
"p": 21,
"text": "You can't hunt with it.",
"start": 260.491,
"end": 261.532
},
{
"i": 75,
"p": 21,
"text": "But everyone wants it.",
"start": 262.822,
"end": 263.991
},
{
"i": 76,
"p": 22,
"text": "Beads like these could show who you were, who you belonged to... and who owed you.",
"start": 265.763,
"end": 271.36
},
{
"i": 77,
"p": 22,
"text": "Some researchers think valuables like this were humanity's first store of wealth.",
"start": 272.622,
"end": 277.414
},
{
"i": 78,
"p": 23,
"text": "Chapter four.",
"start": 279.172,
"end": 280.078
},
{
"i": 79,
"p": 23,
"text": "The clay.",
"start": 281.271,
"end": 281.936
},
{
"i": 80,
"p": 24,
"text": "Now jump forward to Mesopotamia, in what is now Iraq.",
"start": 283.608,
"end": 287.335
},
{
"i": 81,
"p": 24,
"text": "About five thousand years ago.",
"start": 288.643,
"end": 290.424
},
{
"i": 82,
"p": 25,
"text": "People have started farming.",
"start": 292.207,
"end": 293.675
},
{
"i": 83,
"p": 25,
"text": "There are cities, temples, and huge storehouses full of barley.",
"start": 294.999,
"end": 298.684
},
{
"i": 84,
"p": 25,
"text": "And now there's a new problem.",
"start": 300.069,
"end": 301.711
},
{
"i": 85,
"p": 25,
"text": "Who gave what?",
"start": 303.073,
"end": 303.989
},
{
"i": 86,
"p": 25,
"text": "Who owes what?",
"start": 305.217,
"end": 306.212
},
{
"i": 87,
"p": 26,
"text": "So people pressed little marks into wet clay.",
"start": 307.906,
"end": 310.474
},
{
"i": 88,
"p": 26,
"text": "Sheep.",
"start": 311.809,
"end": 312.334
},
{
"i": 89,
"p": 26,
"text": "Barley.",
"start": 313.401,
"end": 313.946
},
{
"i": 90,
"p": 26,
"text": "Oil.",
"start": 315.133,
"end": 315.609
},
{
"i": 91,
"p": 26,
"text": "Debts.",
"start": 316.765,
"end": 317.3
},
{
"i": 92,
"p": 27,
"text": "Here's the amazing part.",
"start": 318.868,
"end": 320.304
},
{
"i": 93,
"p": 27,
"text": "Some of the oldest writing in the world isn't poetry or prayers.",
"start": 321.571,
"end": 325.611
},
{
"i": 94,
"p": 27,
"text": "It's accounting.",
"start": 326.871,
"end": 327.807
},
{
"i": 95,
"p": 27,
"text": "Receipts.",
"start": 329.01,
"end": 329.732
},
{
"i": 96,
"p": 27,
"text": "I-O-Us.",
"start": 330.918,
"end": 331.686
},
{
"i": 97,
"p": 28,
"text": "Money had a unit now.",
"start": 333.343,
"end": 334.716
},
{
"i": 98,
"p": 28,
"text": "A shekel of silver, about eight grams.",
"start": 336.003,
"end": 338.353
},
{
"i": 99,
"p": 28,
"text": "But most people never even touched the silver.",
"start": 339.62,
"end": 342.342
},
{
"i": 100,
"p": 28,
"text": "They ran a tab.",
"start": 343.687,
"end": 344.682
},
{
"i": 101,
"p": 28,
"text": "The tab was the money.",
"start": 345.956,
"end": 347.262
},
{
"i": 102,
"p": 29,
"text": "And just like today, there was interest.",
"start": 349.035,
"end": 351.191
},
{
"i": 103,
"p": 29,
"text": "Around seventeen fifty B C, King Hammurabi's laws capped interest on loans.",
"start": 352.474,
"end": 357.746
},
{
"i": 104,
"p": 29,
"text": "Twenty percent on silver.",
"start": 359.044,
"end": 360.585
},
{
"i": 105,
"p": 29,
"text": "Thirty-three percent on barley.",
"start": 361.917,
"end": 363.62
},
{
"i": 106,
"p": 30,
"text": "And when too many farmers fell into debt, some kings did something you'll never see today.",
"start": 365.462,
"end": 370.564
},
{
"i": 107,
"p": 30,
"text": "They declared a clean slate.",
"start": 371.927,
"end": 373.306
},
{
"i": 108,
"p": 30,
"text": "Certain debts... simply erased.",
"start": 374.68,
"end": 377.805
},
{
"i": 109,
"p": 31,
"text": "Chapter five.",
"start": 379.458,
"end": 380.413
},
{
"i": 110,
"p": 31,
"text": "Cows, shells and salt.",
"start": 381.652,
"end": 383.049
},
{
"i": 111,
"p": 32,
"text": "Around the world, people found their own things to count value with.",
"start": 384.797,
"end": 388.692
},
{
"i": 112,
"p": 33,
"text": "Cattle were so important that the Latin word for money, pecunia, comes from pecus... the word for cattle.",
"start": 390.417,
"end": 398.486
},
{
"i": 113,
"p": 34,
"text": "In ancient China and across Africa and Asia, people used cowrie shells.",
"start": 400.236,
"end": 404.892
},
{
"i": 114,
"p": 34,
"text": "Small, shiny, hard to fake.",
"start": 406.143,
"end": 407.8
},
{
"i": 115,
"p": 34,
"text": "The Chinese character for money and wealth still contains the sign for a shell.",
"start": 409.097,
"end": 413.525
},
{
"i": 116,
"p": 35,
"text": "And salt?",
"start": 415.348,
"end": 416.073
},
{
"i": 117,
"p": 35,
"text": "You've probably heard that Roman soldiers were paid in salt... and that's where the word salary comes from.",
"start": 417.277,
"end": 424.021
},
{
"i": 118,
"p": 35,
"text": "Historians say there's actually very little evidence for that.",
"start": 425.411,
"end": 428.821
},
{
"i": 119,
"p": 35,
"text": "Another myth.",
"start": 430.238,
"end": 431.103
},
{
"i": 120,
"p": 36,
"text": "Chapter six.",
"start": 432.747,
"end": 433.703
},
{
"i": 121,
"p": 36,
"text": "The stones of Yap.",
"start": 434.887,
"end": 436.154
},
{
"i": 122,
"p": 37,
"text": "Now for the strangest money in the world.",
"start": 437.863,
"end": 440.017
},
{
"i": 123,
"p": 38,
"text": "On the tiny Pacific island of Yap, people used giant stone discs as money.",
"start": 441.78,
"end": 446.675
},
{
"i": 124,
"p": 38,
"text": "They're called rai.",
"start": 448.071,
"end": 449.024
},
{
"i": 125,
"p": 38,
"text": "Pronounced rye.",
"start": 450.307,
"end": 451.229
},
{
"i": 126,
"p": 38,
"text": "The biggest is about twelve feet wide and weighs around four tons.",
"start": 452.502,
"end": 456.723
},
{
"i": 127,
"p": 39,
"text": "They were carved on another island, Palau, hundreds of miles away, and brought back by canoe.",
"start": 458.505,
"end": 464.166
},
{
"i": 128,
"p": 40,
"text": "You can't put a four-ton stone in your pocket.",
"start": 466.049,
"end": 468.246
},
{
"i": 129,
"p": 40,
"text": "So the stones almost never moved.",
"start": 469.591,
"end": 471.595
},
{
"i": 130,
"p": 40,
"text": "When one was used as payment, everyone simply remembered that it now had a new owner.",
"start": 472.901,
"end": 478.037
},
{
"i": 131,
"p": 41,
"text": "And here's the best part.",
"start": 479.878,
"end": 481.191
},
{
"i": 132,
"p": 41,
"text": "One giant stone fell into the sea during the trip home.",
"start": 482.514,
"end": 485.392
},
{
"i": 133,
"p": 41,
"text": "It sank to the bottom.",
"start": 486.816,
"end": 488.039
},
{
"i": 134,
"p": 41,
"text": "Nobody ever saw it again.",
"start": 489.32,
"end": 491.008
},
{
"i": 135,
"p": 42,
"text": "But everyone agreed it was still there.",
"start": 492.844,
"end": 494.709
},
{
"i": 136,
"p": 42,
"text": "So it kept being used as money.",
"start": 496.035,
"end": 497.716
},
{
"i": 137,
"p": 42,
"text": "For generations.",
"start": 499.076,
"end": 500.153
},
{
"i": 138,
"p": 42,
"text": "A fortune that nobody could see... that everyone believed in.",
"start": 501.418,
"end": 506.044
},
{
"i": 139,
"p": 43,
"text": "Chapter seven.",
"start": 507.798,
"end": 508.759
},
{
"i": 140,
"p": 43,
"text": "The first coins.",
"start": 509.951,
"end": 511.023
},
{
"i": 141,
"p": 44,
"text": "Coins finally arrive about two thousand six hundred years ago, in the kingdom of Lydia, in modern-day Turkey.",
"start": 512.732,
"end": 519.841
},
{
"i": 142,
"p": 44,
"text": "Little lumps of electrum, a natural mix of gold and silver, stamped with a lion.",
"start": 521.264,
"end": 526.735
},
{
"i": 143,
"p": 45,
"text": "Why then?",
"start": 528.607,
"end": 529.296
},
{
"i": 144,
"p": 45,
"text": "Armies.",
"start": 530.573,
"end": 531.214
},
{
"i": 145,
"p": 45,
"text": "Kings needed to pay soldiers, and soldiers needed something they could carry and spend anywhere.",
"start": 532.424,
"end": 538.011
},
{
"i": 146,
"p": 46,
"text": "Coins spread fast.",
"start": 539.857,
"end": 541.057
},
{
"i": 147,
"p": 46,
"text": "But the old idea of money as a record never went away.",
"start": 542.285,
"end": 545.968
},
{
"i": 148,
"p": 47,
"text": "In England, for more than six hundred years, the government kept track of taxes and debts with wooden tally sticks.",
"start": 547.842,
"end": 555.138
},
{
"i": 149,
"p": 47,
"text": "Notches cut into willow.",
"start": 556.387,
"end": 557.831
},
{
"i": 150,
"p": 47,
"text": "The stick was split in half.",
"start": 559.075,
"end": 560.433
},
{
"i": 151,
"p": 47,
"text": "You kept one half.",
"start": 561.685,
"end": 562.822
},
{
"i": 152,
"p": 47,
"text": "The government kept the other.",
"start": 564.023,
"end": 565.533
},
{
"i": 153,
"p": 48,
"text": "In eighteen thirty-four, officials decided to get rid of the old sticks.",
"start": 567.293,
"end": 571.813
},
{
"i": 154,
"p": 48,
"text": "So they burned them in a furnace under the House of Lords.",
"start": 573.074,
"end": 576.328
},
{
"i": 155,
"p": 49,
"text": "The fire got out of control.",
"start": 578.04,
"end": 579.591
},
{
"i": 156,
"p": 49,
"text": "And it burned down most of the Houses of Parliament.",
"start": 580.933,
"end": 583.618
},
{
"i": 157,
"p": 50,
"text": "Chapter eight.",
"start": 585.462,
"end": 586.325
},
{
"i": 158,
"p": 50,
"text": "Your stone at the bottom of the sea.",
"start": 587.543,
"end": 589.23
},
{
"i": 159,
"p": 51,
"text": "So what did ancient humans use before money?",
"start": 591.037,
"end": 593.562
},
{
"i": 160,
"p": 51,
"text": "Friendship.",
"start": 594.927,
"end": 595.653
},
{
"i": 161,
"p": 51,
"text": "Gifts.",
"start": 596.787,
"end": 597.393
},
{
"i": 162,
"p": 51,
"text": "Shells.",
"start": 598.488,
"end": 599.121
},
{
"i": 163,
"p": 51,
"text": "Clay.",
"start": 600.318,
"end": 600.831
},
{
"i": 164,
"p": 51,
"text": "Cows.",
"start": 602.007,
"end": 602.516
},
{
"i": 165,
"p": 51,
"text": "Giant stones.",
"start": 603.659,
"end": 604.651
},
{
"i": 166,
"p": 51,
"text": "And promises.",
"start": 605.9,
"end": 606.865
},
{
"i": 167,
"p": 52,
"text": "Now look at your phone again.",
"start": 608.563,
"end": 609.986
},
{
"i": 168,
"p": 52,
"text": "Look at that balance.",
"start": 611.315,
"end": 612.513
},
{
"i": 169,
"p": 53,
"text": "There's no gold behind it.",
"start": 614.209,
"end": 615.712
},
{
"i": 170,
"p": 53,
"text": "Most of it isn't even paper.",
"start": 617.078,
"end": 618.823
},
{
"i": 171,
"p": 53,
"text": "It's a number in someone's computer.",
"start": 620.128,
"end": 622.17
},
{
"i": 172,
"p": 53,
"text": "A record.",
"start": 623.511,
"end": 624.211
},
{
"i": 173,
"p": 53,
"text": "A promise that everyone agrees to believe in.",
"start": 625.423,
"end": 628.001
},
{
"i": 174,
"p": 54,
"text": "Just like the stone at the bottom of the sea.",
"start": 629.889,
"end": 632.084
},
{
"i": 175,
"p": 55,
"text": "The people of Yap would understand your bank account perfectly.",
"start": 633.911,
"end": 637.111
},
{
"i": 176,
"p": 55,
"text": "Your ancestors in the Kalahari would find it strange.",
"start": 638.495,
"end": 641.951
},
{
"i": 177,
"p": 55,
"text": "They kept their wealth in people, not numbers.",
"start": 643.234,
"end": 645.973
},
{
"i": 178,
"p": 56,
"text": "Maybe that's the oldest money of all.",
"start": 647.682,
"end": 649.666
},
{
"i": 179,
"p": 57,
"text": "If you enjoyed this file, subscribe to The Money Files.",
"start": 651.44,
"end": 654.946
},
{
"i": 180,
"p": 57,
"text": "Next time: what did a medieval peasant actually earn?",
"start": 656.236,
"end": 661.2
}
]
}